  "prior_source": "P3_reaction_event_law_completed_1927_20261024.json remains unchanged with its original not-retrieved-script status.",
  "normal_source_statements": [
    {
      "text": "I’ll preserve the working files that are still present and make them available through this chat. I’ll then develop the reaction-event construction at its current boundary: what information must pass through a reaction interface when an event leaves part of the molecular state unknown.",
      "html": "<div data-selected-text-overlay-target=\"_r_32u_\" dir=\"auto\" data-markdown-text-style=\"assistant-message\" data-markdown-text-tone=\"primary\" class=\"MarkdownRoot-rZKhxa [&amp;&gt;*:first-child]:mt-0 [&amp;&gt;*:last-child]:mb-0 [&amp;&gt;*:last-child]:mb-0 [&amp;&gt;ol:first-child]:mt-0 [&amp;&gt;ul:first-child]:mt-0\" style=\"--markdown-link-color: currentColor;\"><p class=\"Paragraph-kKnbIo\" dir=\"auto\"><span>I’ll preserve the working files that are still present and make them available through this chat. I’ll then develop the reaction-event construction at its current boundary: what information must pass through a reaction interface when an event leaves part of the molecular state unknown.</span></p></div>"
    },
    {
      "text": "The three scripts and two saved calculation outputs are still present. I’ll preserve those versions.\n\nA useful extension has emerged: successive reactions can inspect different, overlapping subsets of a molecule while carrying a fixed-size mixture that preserves hidden correlations. In a new check, replacing that entrance mixture by independent marginals changed the next reaction’s likelihood by about 5.7%. I’m also developing a broader construction for reactions coupled through a small hidden regulator.",
      "html": "<div data-selected-text-overlay-target=\"_r_34e_\" dir=\"auto\" data-markdown-text-style=\"assistant-message\" data-markdown-text-tone=\"primary\" class=\"MarkdownRoot-rZKhxa [&amp;&gt;*:first-child]:mt-0 [&amp;&gt;*:last-child]:mb-0 [&amp;&gt;*:last-child]:mb-0 [&amp;&gt;ol:first-child]:mt-0 [&amp;&gt;ul:first-child]:mt-0\" style=\"--markdown-link-color: currentColor;\"><p class=\"Paragraph-kKnbIo\" dir=\"auto\"><span>The three scripts and two saved calculation outputs are still present. I’ll preserve those versions.</span></p><p class=\"Paragraph-kKnbIo\" dir=\"auto\"><span>A useful extension has emerged: successive reactions can inspect different, overlapping subsets of a molecule while carrying a fixed-size mixture that preserves hidden correlations. In a new check, replacing that entrance mixture by independent marginals changed the next reaction’s likelihood by about 5.7%. I’m also developing a broader construction for reactions coupled through a small hidden regulator.</span></p></div>"
    },
    {
      "text": "The preserved scripts are available here: \npro3_gate_experiment.py, \nrare_green_certificate.py, and \nquery_gate_checks.py.\n\nThe new construction now accounts for finite timestamp resolution. Uncertainty about when a reaction occurred can itself correlate untouched sites. I’m implementing a positive-mixture approximation whose error is bounded for the whole entrance distribution, so the same certificate remains useful for later reaction queries.",
